purpose: The engine side of the seam. Owns the page; produces display lists, never pixels.

# Notably absent: platform. The engine has no window, no renderer, and no way
# to acquire one. That is what makes moving it into a sandboxed process a
# scheduling decision rather than a rewrite.
# `bindings` and not `js`: the engine owns the interpreter but reaches the
# document through the binding layer like everything else, so there is still
# exactly one path from script to the tree. See docs/adr/0008-dom-bindings.md.
allow: util gfx ipc url privacy net dom html css layout js bindings

public: Engine.h Loader.h Page.h PendingLoad.h

extern:

max_tu_lines: 800

# Engine is the class most at risk of becoming "the browser". Document,
# navigation history, network, and script each get their own type; Engine stays
# the thing that routes messages to them. This budget is the tripwire.
# Grew by two methods for the timers: how long the loop may sleep, and running
# what is due. Both are queries the loop makes of the page rather than new
# state on Engine, which is why the member count did not move.
# Grew for ADR 0011. The loop now asks the engine four questions it did not
# have to ask when a load blocked: carry it forward, what do I wait on, is
# anything runnable right now, and are you still loading. Those are the loop's
# questions rather than the browser's, which is why they are on the router.
# The ninth member is the navigation in flight -- predicted by name in ADR 0011,
# and a value of its own type so that Engine gained one member and not eight.
budget: Engine header_lines=115 public_methods=13 members=9
# The network half, kept off Engine on purpose: policy, sockets, cookies and
# cache are four members that would otherwise be four more reasons for Engine
# to become the browser.
# Started, advanced, and asked what it is waiting on: six of these methods are
# the shape ADR 0011 imposed, and the two that used to return a document are
# gone. Members did not move -- the transport pointer and the owned block
# reason were replaced by the request queue and the answers it needs no network
# for.
budget: Loader header_lines=105 public_methods=14 members=6
# One document: DOM, styles, box tree, and the display list they produce. This
# is the unit a second tab duplicates.
# Member 9 is the document resource plan: linked stylesheet slots, image URLs,
# and decoded image pixels. Keeping those in one value matters because external
# stylesheets are fetched by the loader but cascade in document order with
# inline <style> elements.
# Member 10 is the original form-control state, keyed by element, so reset controls can
# restore DOM-mutated values without smuggling internal attributes into the DOM.
# Member 11 is the currently focused text-control element, a non-owning pointer
# into the document so form editing state lives on the DOM value rather than in
# a second parallel store. Page collects the URLs and the Loader fetches them,
# because resolving a relative href against a base URL is the loader's job and
# parsing is this one's.
# Grew by one for the page's script. One member and not two: an interpreter
# and a binding layer would have taken Page's fan-out to six modules, which the
# lint refused -- correctly, since Page is the class most at risk of becoming
# "the browser". PageScript owns that pair; Page coordinates it.
# One navigation, from asked-for to on-screen. A struct with predicates rather
# than a class: there is no invariant to protect, and the engine is what acts on
# it. See ADR 0011.
# Sixteen fields, and the two newest are the point of the ADR 0011 script
# decision: an `async` script is counted apart from the rest and the first
# frame is a state the load passes through rather than the state it ends in.
budget: PendingLoad header_lines=70 public_methods=6 members=16

budget: Page   header_lines=170 public_methods=36 members=12
# The script half of a page: the interpreter, the binding layer, and the
# scripts themselves in document order.
#
# Four of the six members are that ordering problem, and they are the reason
# this is a class rather than a function: a page's scripts must run in the
# order they appear whether each is inline or external, so nothing can run
# until every external one has arrived. A slot per script keeps the order, a
# parallel list says which slots are still owed, and a flag makes Run
# idempotent so a caller that fetches and one that does not can both end with
# it. The stylesheets solve the same problem the same way.
# Seven members: the interpreter, the binding layer, the four that keep the
# scripts in document order, and the timer queue. The timers are here rather
# than on Page because they belong to the script that scheduled them and go
# with it on the next navigation.
# Eight, for the errors a script threw. Run deliberately continues past a
# throw, so without somewhere to put them a page whose every script failed and
# a page with no script at all render identically and report the same thing --
# which is exactly the position youtube.com was diagnosed from, and the reason
# the eighth member is worth more than the budget it costs.
# Grew for the three script timings. Two more methods -- is this one the page
# waits for, and run the ones that landed late -- and the same eight members:
# the slot became a struct rather than the timings becoming three more
# vectors. See ADR 0011.
budget: PageScript header_lines=145 public_methods=17 members=8
